---
id: AP-008
type: rule
family: dynamic-execution
title: eval() called with a dynamic argument
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 1

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist: ['**/*.test.ts', '**/*.spec.ts', '**/__tests__/**', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\beval\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_$]'

related: [AP-009]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

Don't call `eval()` with a dynamic argument. The first character
inside the parens is an identifier (a variable, function call, or
property access), which means the string being executed is not
visible to static analysis and may be attacker-controlled at
runtime.

Replace with a structured dispatch — a `Record<string, () =>
Result>` map keyed on the input, a Zod / ArkType schema for data
validation, or a sandboxed isolate (`vm.runInContext` with a fresh
context + timeout, QuickJS-emscripten, a Worker) if you genuinely
need to run untrusted code.

If the argument is a string literal you control end-to-end (e.g.
`eval("1 + 1")` in a one-off benchmark), the rule is intentionally
narrow enough to skip you: the leading character inside the parens
is a quote, not an identifier. That keeps the false-positive rate
low for the rare benign case while still catching the
arbitrary-code-execution shape.
